#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11002" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\cos x$? Assume that $n$ is an integer.\n\n1. The range of the function is $[-1,1]$.\n2. The function has minimum values at $x =n\\pi$.\n3. The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +\\pi n$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's plot the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3557/Source/Images/57d6cfd2524b25259c227c929e2b4545.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The minimum value is $-1$, and the maximum value is $1$. Therefore, the range is $[-1,1]$.\n- Statement II is false. The function has a minimum at $x=\\pi$ and the period is $2\\pi$. Therefore, the minimum values occur at $\\pi+2n\\pi$.\n- Statement III is true. These are the zeros of the cosine curve. The cosine curve has zeros at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11003" :question/problem "If $\\cos x =-\\dfrac {1}{3}$, find the value of $2\\cos(x + 4\\pi)+3$." :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\cos x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} = \\cos{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=2$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos x &= \\cos (x + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\cos (x + 4π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\cos{x} =-\\dfrac {1}{3}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\cos{(x+4\\pi)} =-\\dfrac {1}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\cos (x + 4π) + 3 &= 2(-\\frac{1}{3}) + 3 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3} + 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175627 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-11012" :question/problem "If $\\cos x = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}$, then find the value of $\\cos (-x-6\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\cos{x}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3557/Source/Images/dfab92fbee88df2003b54abc658b5d81.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\cos{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\cos{x}$ is an *even* function, and we have\n$\\cos(-x) = \\cos{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\cos{x}$, we have\n$\\cos (-x - 6π)|= \\cos (-x - 2π \\cdot 3); = \\cos (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\cos{x}$, we have\n$\\cos (-x)|= \\cos x; = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\cos (-x-6\\pi)= \\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e2c8de2-8e98-5c82-87a4-353110263110" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174271} {:answer/id #uuid "e857146a-3e93-51a1-89a9-e74550fe2100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174272} {:answer/id #uuid "59f8ed8e-ae4f-509e-9a2d-3fdd6f146471" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174273} {:answer/id #uuid "77607a6c-5be9-5b0a-9cf3-4d779a887700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174274} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9edf4b-963f-5298-bd0d-647e98728407" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77607a6c-5be9-5b0a-9cf3-4d779a887700" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174274} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15cc57f-a06e-5f3f-bc05-daabb0d923f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174270}] :question/id #uuid "195eeaaa-ad0f-5b04-bea4-14b7076ff8c6" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-136545" :question/problem "If $\\cos a =-\\frac{1}{3}$, then $\\cos (-a) =$"}]}
